        "details": "The central simulation sets the saving from AI at two person-hours per initial requirement unit and keeps the retained fraction unchanged. To declare a benefit, a trial must establish a saving greater than one hour per unit and rule out a retention loss of five percentage points or more. Of 20,000 trials at each size, 8,957 met both criteria with 48 teams and 15,450 did so with 96 teams. In the larger design, 4,541 of the 4,550 trials that fell short could not establish the required labour saving."
      }
    }
    require role == "grounds"
  }

  evidence backing_material {
    kind argument_material
    environment engineering_work
    max_age 315360000
    derive material
    input {
      "material": {
        "role": "backing",
        "classification": "Monte Carlo design evaluation and one-parameter sensitivity analyses",
        "details": "In each simulated trial, a fair coin toss determines which team in each pair receives the offer. Each team has two underlying standard normal values, one for labour and one for retention, with correlation 0.3. Values for the same outcome within a pair have correlation 0.7. An underlying labour value z gives baseline hours per initial unit of 3 + 7 exp(0.35 z − 0.35²/2). For retention the corresponding formula is 0.72 + 0.25/(1 + exp(−1.4 z)). The simulator adds the stipulated effects to the paired differences and then adds independent, zero-mean normal measurement errors. Their standard deviations are sqrt(2) times 0.2 hours per initial unit for labour and sqrt(2) times 0.005 for the retained fraction. Every pair supplies both outcomes in the central scenario. The one-sided paired Student t bounds allow 0.2 hours per initial unit and one percentage point for differences in bias between groups. With 96 teams, 15,459 trials passed the labour criterion and 19,990 passed the retention criterion."
      }
    }
    require role == "backing"
  }

  evidence scope_conditions {
    kind argument_material
    environment engineering_work
    max_age 315360000
    derive material
    input {
      "material": {
        "role": "scope",
        "classification": "Conditions for applying this inference",
        "details": "The simulator generates outcomes for whole teams. The planned twenty tasks per team enter only the collection budget. These runs provide no task-level forecasts. Repeating the simulation can reduce the sampling error in its detection rates. Establishing how closely the simulated teams resemble actual teams requires workplace measurements."
      }
    }
    require role == "scope"
  }

  assumption applicability {
    statement "The simulator generates outcomes for whole teams. The planned twenty tasks per team enter only the collection budget. These runs provide no task-level forecasts. Repeating the simulation can reduce the sampling error in its detection rates. Establishing how closely the simulated teams resemble actual teams requires workplace measurements."
    environment engineering_work
    validate scope_conditions
  }

  reasoning warrant {
    method "structured/1"
    rationale "The saving is known in each simulated trial, so the proportion meeting both criteria estimates the chance of detecting that saving under the chosen assumptions. The design requires the lower 95% Monte Carlo limit to reach 90% in every required benefit scenario. Even the central estimates, 44.79% and 77.25%, fall below 90%."
