        "role": "backing",
        "classification": "Monte Carlo bias stress test and constructed task-level example",
        "details": "The simulation draws standard normal values for labour and retention with correlation 0.3 within a team and 0.7 within a pair for the same outcome. A labour value z gives baseline hours per initial unit of 3 + 7 exp(0.35 z − 0.35²/2). For retention the corresponding formula is 0.72 + 0.25/(1 + exp(−1.4 z)). Each pair provides both outcomes. The paired differences include independent normal measurement errors with standard deviations of sqrt(2) times 0.2 hours per initial unit for labour and sqrt(2) times 0.005 for retention. A separate worked example traces the bias to individual tasks. Each of twelve teams has two fixed requirements. Accurate measurements put both mean effects exactly at their thresholds, so the rule withholds a benefit declaration. Omitting one reviewer-hour per initial unit from the AI teams’ hours raises their apparent saving to two hours per unit. Counting six unfulfilled criteria per hundred as satisfied turns the five-point loss into an apparent one-point gain. Those two measurement errors produce a benefit declaration for the same work."
      }
    }
    require role == "backing"
  }

  evidence scope_conditions {
    kind argument_material
    environment engineering_work
    max_age 315360000
    derive material
    input {
      "material": {
        "role": "scope",
        "classification": "Conditions for applying this inference",
        "details": "The false-declaration rates of 47.81% and 76.21% apply to the specified biases and simulated teams. Measuring how often comparable errors occur in a workplace, and how large they are, requires an independent estimate of omitted time and wrongly scored requirements."
      }
    }
    require role == "scope"
  }

  assumption applicability {
    statement "The false-declaration rates of 47.81% and 76.21% apply to the specified biases and simulated teams. Measuring how often comparable errors occur in a workplace, and how large they are, requires an independent estimate of omitted time and wrongly scored requirements."
    environment engineering_work
    validate scope_conditions
  }

  reasoning warrant {
    method "structured/1"
    rationale "When measurement errors consistently favour one group, more independent pairs can reduce the sampling uncertainty around a biased mean difference. The adjusted bounds can then cross the decision thresholds more often even though the underlying effects remain at those thresholds."
    backing [backing_material]
  }

  claim finding {
    statement "A larger trial can declare a benefit falsely more often when systematic measurement errors favour AI by more than the analysis allows."
    environment engineering_work
  }

  claim exception_applies {
    statement "Applying this result to a particular workforce requires evidence that differences in measurement bias between the two groups could exceed the allowances."
    environment engineering_work
  }

  // Time reviews independently and test whether the software satisfies the follow-up
  // criteria. These checks can bound the difference in measurement bias between groups.
  // If plausible corrections take either outcome across its threshold, collecting more
  // teams’ reports alone cannot settle the decision.
  objection challenge = [premises exception_applies] -x> argument inference

  argument inference = [
    evidence grounds,
    assumptions applicability,
    premises arg_16.finding
  ] via warrant => finding
}

module arg_19 {
  evidence grounds {
    kind argument_material
    environment engineering_work
    max_age 315360000
    derive material
    input {
      "material": {
        "role": "grounds",
        "classification": "Probability bound, exact binomial limit and conditional simulation",
        "details": "Every team must supply both its post-intake labour per initial requirement unit and the fraction of its initial score retained. If each pair independently has a 0.5% chance of lacking either outcome, the chance of complete data is 88.7% with 24 pairs and 78.6% with 48 pairs. These upper limits on the chance of declaring a benefit both fall below the 90% detection target. Of 20,000 simulated trials with 48 pairs, 4,212 lacked an outcome and could not establish a benefit."
      }
    }
    require role == "grounds"
  }

  evidence backing_material {
    kind argument_material
    environment engineering_work
    max_age 315360000
    derive material
    input {
      "material": {
        "role": "backing",
        "classification": "Probability bound, exact binomial limit and conditional simulation",
        "details": "If each pair independently has probability q of lacking an outcome, the probability that six pairs all supply complete data is (1−q)^6. Setting this probability to 0.05 gives an exact one-sided 95% upper limit on q of 1−0.05^(1/6), about 39.3%. Even six complete pairs would therefore leave plausible loss probabilities far above 0.5%."
      }
    }
    require role == "backing"
  }

  evidence scope_conditions {
    kind argument_material
    environment engineering_work
    max_age 315360000
    derive material
    input {
      "material": {
        "role": "scope",
        "classification": "Conditions for applying this inference",
        "details": "These numerical ceilings assume that the pairs lose outcomes independently at the same rate and that the decision requires every outcome. The proposed six-pair feasibility study gives each team six initial tasks. It can measure time spent reporting and identify why labour measurements or follow-up assessments are unavailable."
      }
    }
    require role == "scope"
  }

  assumption applicability {
    statement "These numerical ceilings assume that the pairs lose outcomes independently at the same rate and that the decision requires every outcome. The proposed six-pair feasibility study gives each team six initial tasks. It can measure time spent reporting and identify why labour measurements or follow-up assessments are unavailable."
    environment engineering_work
    validate scope_conditions
  }

  reasoning warrant {
    method "structured/1"
    rationale "If each of m independent pairs supplies both outcomes with probability 1−q, all m do so with probability (1−q)^m. Even a trial that detects the true benefit whenever its data are complete can declare a benefit only that often."
    backing [backing_material]
  }

  claim finding {
    statement "Requiring both outcomes from every pair limits the chance of establishing a benefit. Six pairs provide too little evidence to establish that losses are rare."
    environment engineering_work
  }

  claim exception_applies {
    statement "Dependent losses change the chance of complete data. A rule that permits decisions with incomplete pairs also changes the upper limit on the chance of establishing a benefit."
    environment engineering_work
  }

  // Suppose a single outage removes outcomes for every pair with probability 0.5% and
  // all outcomes are otherwise available. Each pair still has a 0.5% chance of loss, yet
  // both trial sizes have a 99.5% chance of complete data. A procedure using partial
  // outcomes would need prespecified bounds and an evaluation of its error rates.
  objection challenge = [premises exception_applies] -x> argument inference

  argument inference = [
    evidence grounds,
    assumptions applicability,
    premises arg_16.finding
  ] via warrant => finding
}
